{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012anjCgz6PvfLeczQvy3QWN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016qTfa5WJ7EXvtPwdsm26dN","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. \n\nCoRL rating scale (1-10): 1=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 5=borderline reject, 6=borderline accept, 8=strong accept, 10=top 5%. Acceptance rate ~25-30%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper systematically analyzes why inference-time vision token pruning works on some VLA architectures but catastrophically fails on others. Testing 5 VLA models (π0 3.3B, X-VLA 880M, OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B, SmolVLA 450M) spanning 3 vision connector families on LIBERO, they find:\n1. Pure linear (π0): pruning-immune (loss improves 2.4% at 90% removal)\n2. Linear + BART 12L (X-VLA): threshold-graded (97.6% SR at k=0.9, cliff to 56.2% at k=0.7)\n3. 3-layer MLP (OpenVLA): smooth graded degradation (-49% relative at 50% removal)\n4. Pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA): catastrophic (0% SR at 5% removal)\n\nCV of token importance scores predicts this spectrum (0.038→0.151→0.215→0.314). For pixel-shuffle, they propose VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding, ~22M params) recovering 0%→60%+ SR at 75% compression. VTR fails on MLP-projector (0% SR), reinforcing connector-dependency.\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: Cross-architecture taxonomy (5 models, CV, SR at k=0.9/0.7/0.5)\n- Table 2: SmolVLA all-zero pruning across 4 methods (Random/ACIS/ToMe/Sobel)\n- Table 3: OpenVLA degradation curve, random vs L2-norm (Δ<3pp)\n- Table 4: X-VLA L2-norm pruning with seed44 replication at cliff point\n- Table 5: SmolVLA VTR M-sweep (3-seed), flat M-curve M=16 to M=128\n- Table 6: VTR/VTC-only on OpenVLA — all fail (0-7% SR)\n- Table 7: Diagnostic metrics (entropy + CV) across 5 models\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper covers experiments that are largely complete. Some experiments still running at submission time:\n- OpenVLA VTC-only M=32/64/96 (predicted 2-7% SR) — these appear in Table 6\n- OpenVLA L2-norm pruning full sweep (predicted Δ<3pp vs random) — appears in Table 3\n- X-VLA k=0.7 seed44 and k=0.9 seed44 — appears in Table 4\n- OpenVLA random k=0.7 seed43, k=0.5 seed43 — mentioned in Section 4.3\n- RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler) — NOT completed, mentioned as future work\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental methodology soundness**\n   - Is the pruning protocol well-controlled? (retention ratios, multiple pruning methods)\n   - Are baselines fair and sufficient?\n   - Is LIBERO an appropriate benchmark? Is single-benchmark evaluation sufficient?\n   - Statistical rigor: 500 episodes, 3 seeds for VTR, 2 seeds for X-VLA cliff — adequate?\n\n2. **Controlled comparisons**\n   - π0 vs OpenVLA as connector isolation (same token count, same SigLIP lineage)\n   - Random vs L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA\n   - VTR on SmolVLA vs VTR on OpenVLA (connector-dependency of fix)\n   - Seed replications\n\n3. **VTR method**\n   - Is the M-sweep convincing? (M=16 to M=128, flat curve)\n   - M=128 passthrough as re-encoding isolation — is this a valid control?\n   - MLP adapter comparison (architecture independence, p=0.15)\n   - Cross-suite generalization (Object→Spatial/Goal)\n   - VTR on OpenVLA failure — well-explained?\n\n4. **Missing experiments / weaknesses**\n   - Only LIBERO (simulation) — no real robot\n   - Only 3 connector families confirmed (4th predicted)\n   - π0 evaluated offline only (no closed-loop SR for pruning)\n   - OpenVLA-OFT: no closed-loop pruning results, only entropy\n   - n=3 seeds, Bonferroni concern acknowledged\n   - Some values in tables appear predicted (marked with ~)\n\n5. **Statistical concerns**\n   - 500 episodes × 10 tasks — is this enough statistical power for the claimed differences?\n   - Wilson CI mentioned for some comparisons — are the key claims statistically significant?\n   - L2-norm vs random Δ<3pp — is this \"no difference\" or underpowered?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What additional experiments would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any presentation/clarity issues in methods/experiments sections]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: [1-4, where 4=excellent]\n- Empirical Rigor: [1-4]\n- Overall Recommendation: [1-10 CoRL scale]\n- Confidence: [1-5, where 5=expert]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31626,"cache_read_input_tokens":32333,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31626},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885","uuid":"87cb8d75-2317-4541-95d2-3620e9918c93"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"addfec8de037b2d6a","tool_use_id":"toolu_016qTfa5WJ7EXvtPwdsm26dN","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. \n\nCoRL rating scale (1-10): 1=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 5=borderline reject, 6=borderline accept, 8=strong accept, 10=top 5%. Acceptance rate ~25-30%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper systematically analyzes why inference-time vision token pruning works on some VLA architectures but catastrophically fails on others. Testing 5 VLA models (π0 3.3B, X-VLA 880M, OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B, SmolVLA 450M) spanning 3 vision connector families on LIBERO, they find:\n1. Pure linear (π0): pruning-immune (loss improves 2.4% at 90% removal)\n2. Linear + BART 12L (X-VLA): threshold-graded (97.6% SR at k=0.9, cliff to 56.2% at k=0.7)\n3. 3-layer MLP (OpenVLA): smooth graded degradation (-49% relative at 50% removal)\n4. Pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA): catastrophic (0% SR at 5% removal)\n\nCV of token importance scores predicts this spectrum (0.038→0.151→0.215→0.314). For pixel-shuffle, they propose VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding, ~22M params) recovering 0%→60%+ SR at 75% compression. VTR fails on MLP-projector (0% SR), reinforcing connector-dependency.\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: Cross-architecture taxonomy (5 models, CV, SR at k=0.9/0.7/0.5)\n- Table 2: SmolVLA all-zero pruning across 4 methods (Random/ACIS/ToMe/Sobel)\n- Table 3: OpenVLA degradation curve, random vs L2-norm (Δ<3pp)\n- Table 4: X-VLA L2-norm pruning with seed44 replication at cliff point\n- Table 5: SmolVLA VTR M-sweep (3-seed), flat M-curve M=16 to M=128\n- Table 6: VTR/VTC-only on OpenVLA — all fail (0-7% SR)\n- Table 7: Diagnostic metrics (entropy + CV) across 5 models\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper covers experiments that are largely complete. Some experiments still running at submission time:\n- OpenVLA VTC-only M=32/64/96 (predicted 2-7% SR) — these appear in Table 6\n- OpenVLA L2-norm pruning full sweep (predicted Δ<3pp vs random) — appears in Table 3\n- X-VLA k=0.7 seed44 and k=0.9 seed44 — appears in Table 4\n- OpenVLA random k=0.7 seed43, k=0.5 seed43 — mentioned in Section 4.3\n- RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler) — NOT completed, mentioned as future work\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental methodology soundness**\n   - Is the pruning protocol well-controlled? (retention ratios, multiple pruning methods)\n   - Are baselines fair and sufficient?\n   - Is LIBERO an appropriate benchmark? Is single-benchmark evaluation sufficient?\n   - Statistical rigor: 500 episodes, 3 seeds for VTR, 2 seeds for X-VLA cliff — adequate?\n\n2. **Controlled comparisons**\n   - π0 vs OpenVLA as connector isolation (same token count, same SigLIP lineage)\n   - Random vs L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA\n   - VTR on SmolVLA vs VTR on OpenVLA (connector-dependency of fix)\n   - Seed replications\n\n3. **VTR method**\n   - Is the M-sweep convincing? (M=16 to M=128, flat curve)\n   - M=128 passthrough as re-encoding isolation — is this a valid control?\n   - MLP adapter comparison (architecture independence, p=0.15)\n   - Cross-suite generalization (Object→Spatial/Goal)\n   - VTR on OpenVLA failure — well-explained?\n\n4. **Missing experiments / weaknesses**\n   - Only LIBERO (simulation) — no real robot\n   - Only 3 connector families confirmed (4th predicted)\n   - π0 evaluated offline only (no closed-loop SR for pruning)\n   - OpenVLA-OFT: no closed-loop pruning results, only entropy\n   - n=3 seeds, Bonferroni concern acknowledged\n   - Some values in tables appear predicted (marked with ~)\n\n5. **Statistical concerns**\n   - 500 episodes × 10 tasks — is this enough statistical power for the claimed differences?\n   - Wilson CI mentioned for some comparisons — are the key claims statistically significant?\n   - L2-norm vs random Δ<3pp — is this \"no difference\" or underpowered?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What additional experiments would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any presentation/clarity issues in methods/experiments sections]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: [1-4, where 4=excellent]\n- Empirical Rigor: [1-4]\n- Overall Recommendation: [1-10 CoRL scale]\n- Confidence: [1-5, where 5=expert]\n```\n","uuid":"79c583b9-09b7-4367-92f5-b35cec443d79","session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885"}
