{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HYYJSbnazjEnDbz3eURCdV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Methods and Experimental Setup}\n2\t\\label{sec:experimental_setup}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Architectures and Benchmark}\n5\t\\label{sec:setup_overview}\n6\t\n7\tWe study five VLA architectures spanning three vision connector families (Figure~\\ref{fig:architecture}).\n8\t\n9\t\\textbf{Linear projection.} $\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B parameters, 256 tokens) maps SigLIP~\\cite{zhai2023siglip} patches through a linear layer into a PaliGemma backbone and generates actions via flow matching~\\cite{lipman2023flow}. X-VLA~\\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M parameters, 50 tokens) augments its linear projection with a 12-layer BART encoder~\\cite{lewis2020bart}; self-attention in these layers introduces cross-token mixing that makes the effective pipeline more complex than the nominal ``linear projection'' label suggests.\n10\t\n11\t\\textbf{MLP projector.} OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B parameters, 256 tokens) fuses DINOv2~\\cite{oquab2023dinov2} and SigLIP encoders through a three-layer MLP (GELU activations) that maps each patch independently into a Llama-2 backbone. OFT~\\cite{kim2025openoft} shares this architecture with an optimized fine-tuning action head; we include it for attention entropy comparison only.\n12\t\n13\t\\textbf{Spatial folding.} SmolVLA~\\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M parameters, 128 tokens from two cameras) encodes each camera image with SigLIP into 1024 patches, then applies a pixel-shuffle connector that folds each $4{\\times}4$ block into one token by channel concatenation (64 per camera). Each token encodes 16 spatially contiguous patches; removing one eliminates an entire $4{\\times}4$ receptive field. The tokens enter a Qwen2-0.5B backbone~\\cite{yang2024qwen2} with flow matching action generation.\n14\t\n15\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n16\t\\centering\n17\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture.pdf}\n18\t\\caption{Vision connector comparison. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle folds $4{\\times}4$ patches into single tokens (128 total, spatially entangled). OpenVLA's MLP projector maps each patch independently (256 tokens, per-token independence).}\n19\t\\label{fig:architecture}\n20\t\\end{figure}\n21\t\n22\tWe evaluate on LIBERO~\\cite{liu2023libero}, a suite of language-conditioned tabletop manipulation tasks organized into three suites: Object (10 tasks), Spatial (10 tasks), and Goal (10 tasks). Each suite uses 50 episodes per task (500 total), evaluated by closed-loop Success Rate (SR) with Wilson 95\\% confidence intervals. Full fine-tuning baselines on Object: SmolVLA 44.8\\%, OpenVLA 74.0\\%, X-VLA 99.2\\%.\n23\t\n24\t\\subsection{SmolVLA Pruning Ablation}\n25\t\\label{sec:smolvla_ablation_setup}\n26\t\n27\tWe train a lightweight ACIS module (3.46M parameters, 0.77\\% of SmolVLA) to predict per-token importance scores with VLA weights frozen. The ablation varies scoring (learned ACIS vs.\\ random), retention ratio ($k \\in \\{0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging via ToMe~\\cite{bolya2023tome}), yielding seven configurations.\n28\t\n29\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Pruning Protocol}\n30\t\\label{sec:openvla_eval_setup}\n31\t\n32\tCross-model evaluation uses two complementary protocols. \\emph{Offline diagnostic analysis} quantifies token importance structure: for each model, we compute the L2 norm of every vision token, rank tokens by norm, and zero out the bottom $(1{-}k)$ fraction. Offline single-step action loss is recorded across retention ratios. This protocol isolates token heterogeneity because L2 norm ranking preserves the most informative tokens by construction. $\\pi_0$ is evaluated at $k \\in \\{0.1, 0.5\\}$ (normalized L1 action loss). OFT provides diagnostic measurements (attention entropy, token importance CV) without pruning evaluation.\n33\t\n34\t\\emph{Closed-loop evaluation} tests robustness on LIBERO-Object under two pruning strategies that differ in difficulty. OpenVLA uses random token removal (uniform random selection, physical removal of unselected tokens) at $k \\in \\{0.30, 0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\\}$, retaining 30\\%--95\\% of 256 vision tokens. Random removal provides a conservative lower bound because it does not preserve the most informative tokens. X-VLA uses L2 norm-based removal (rank by token norm, physically remove lowest-norm tokens) at $k \\in \\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\\}$. Importance-based removal is a more favorable test because it preferentially retains high-norm tokens. The fact that OpenVLA degrades smoothly under the harder test (random) while X-VLA exhibits a threshold at $k{=}0.7$ under the easier test (importance-based) strengthens the architecture-dependent conclusion. SmolVLA is evaluated under both strategies and multiple mechanisms (zero-masking, true removal, token merging; Section~\\ref{sec:smolvla_ablation_setup}), all producing 0\\% SR.\n35\t\n36\tTwo metrics characterize each model's vision token structure from a single forward pass on task-relevant frames. \\emph{Attention entropy ratio}: normalized Shannon entropy of attention weights over vision tokens, where low entropy indicates concentrated attention on a few tokens. \\emph{Token importance CV}: coefficient of variation of L2 norms across vision tokens, where high CV signals heterogeneous importance distribution.\n37\t\n38\t\\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}\n39\t\\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}\n40\t\n41\tWe insert a cross-attention pooling module (22.2M parameters, $\\sim$5\\% of SmolVLA) between the pixel-shuffle connector and LLM backbone. $M$ learned queries attend over all 128 source tokens, producing $M$ compressed representations. We evaluate $M \\in \\{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\\}$ ($M{=}128$ is a passthrough control). A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) serves as an architecture control. Training details are in Appendix~\\ref{app:smart_compress_details}."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016qTfa5WJ7EXvtPwdsm26dN","session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885","uuid":"c0dab5cd-bc4a-46c8-aa17-b9e00ac21e64","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:46:12.962Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01StkBnT4swZ9Dpb2viTCr1L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NAKBJJaLobMmLsZWCMqn35","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/docs/paper/results.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21884,"cache_read_input_tokens":23862,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":21884,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016qTfa5WJ7EXvtPwdsm26dN","session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885","uuid":"5fa8867d-4f10-4bff-b62c-1113aaaf3dad"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NAKBJJaLobMmLsZWCMqn35","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:results}\n3\t\n4\tWe evaluate token pruning across five VLA models spanning three connector families on LIBERO-Object~\\cite{liu2023libero} (10 tasks, 50 episodes each, 500 total). All success rates include Wilson 95\\% confidence intervals for single-seed results and mean $\\pm$ standard deviation for multi-seed results.\n5\t\n6\t\\subsection{Taxonomy Overview}\n7\t\\label{sec:taxonomy_overview}\n8\t\n9\tTable~\\ref{tab:taxonomy} summarizes the five models and their pruning responses. Effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity, rather than the nominal connector label, determines pruning tolerance: pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) is immune, linear projection + deep encoder (X-VLA) is threshold-graded, MLP degrades smoothly, and pixel-shuffle collapses catastrophically. Token importance CV tracks this ordering continuously (0.038, 0.151, 0.215, 0.314) across a 16$\\times$ parameter range (450M--7B). A controlled comparison between $\\pi_0$ and OpenVLA---both using 256 SigLIP~\\cite{zhai2023siglip} tokens, differing only in connector---yields a 63\\,pp gap at $k{=}0.1$, isolating the pipeline as causal (Section~\\ref{sec:linear_results}).\n10\t\n11\t\\begin{table}[t]\n12\t\\centering\n13\t\\small\n14\t\\caption{Five-model connector taxonomy. $^\\dagger\\pi_0$ reports offline normalized L1 action loss (pending closed-loop evaluation). $^\\ddagger$X-VLA uses a linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder; the effective pipeline is not purely linear. $^\\S$OFT reports attention entropy only (no pruning evaluation); entropy match with standard OpenVLA confirms the connector determines attention structure.}\n15\t\\label{tab:taxonomy}\n16\t\\begin{tabular}{llcrll}\n17\t\\toprule\n18\tModel & Connector & Params & Tokens & Representative Result & Class \\\\\n19\t\\midrule\n20\t$\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024pi0} & Linear proj & 3.3B & 256 & $k{=}0.1$: $\\mathcal{L}_1{=}0.976$ ($+$2.4\\%)$^\\dagger$ & Immune (offline) \\\\\n21\tX-VLA~\\cite{zheng2025xvla} & Linear proj$^\\ddagger$ & 880M & 50 & $k{=}0.9$: $97.8{\\pm}0.4$, $k{=}0.7$: $57.2{\\pm}1.6$ & Thresh.-graded \\\\\n22\tOpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} & 3-layer MLP & 7B & 256 & $k{=}0.5$: 37.4\\% SR ($-$49\\%) & Smooth graded \\\\\n23\tOFT~\\cite{kim2025openoft} & 3-layer MLP & 7B & 256 & Ent.\\ $0.667 \\approx 0.661$ (Std)$^\\S$ & Smooth graded \\\\\n24\tSmolVLA~\\cite{shukor2025smolvla} & Pixel-shuffle & 450M & 128 & $k{=}0.95$: 0\\% SR & Catastrophic cliff \\\\\n25\t\\bottomrule\n26\t\\end{tabular}\n27\t\\end{table}\n28\t\n29\t\\subsection{Pixel-Shuffle: Catastrophic Cliff-Edge}\n30\t\\label{sec:smolvla_results}\n31\t\n32\tThe SmolVLA baseline (bypass mode, all 128 vision tokens) achieves 44.8\\% SR (224/500, CI [40.5\\%, 49.2\\%]).\\footnote{The official SmolVLA result is 87.3\\% SR with batch size 64 on 8 GPUs. Our reproduction uses a smaller batch due to compute constraints.} Every pruning configuration produces 0\\% SR across seven configurations varying scoring (learned ACIS vs.\\ random), retention ratio ($k \\in \\{0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging); full ablation in Table~\\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation}, Appendix~\\ref{app:smolvla_ablation}. The official SmolVLA checkpoint (59.2\\% SR) exhibits identical failure, confirming the cliff-edge is architectural. The pattern generalizes across LIBERO suites (Spatial: 0.6\\%, Goal: 0\\%).\n33\t\n34\t\\subsection{MLP Projector: Graded Degradation}\n35\t\\label{sec:openvla_results}\n36\t\n37\tUnder random token removal, OpenVLA degrades gracefully across all retention ratios: 69.4\\% SR at $k{=}0.95$ ($-4.6$pp from the 74.0\\% baseline), 62.0\\% at $k{=}0.90$, 49.0\\% at $k{=}0.70$, 37.4\\% at $k{=}0.50$, and 20.6\\% at $k{=}0.30$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:results_combined}a; full results with CIs in Table~\\ref{tab:openvla_full}, Appendix). At $k{=}0.30$, two tasks (cream\\_cheese, butter) reach 0\\% SR, but eight retain non-zero performance: degradation is graded, not cliff-edge. Per-task breakdowns are in Appendix~\\ref{app:per_task}. Random token removal provides a conservative lower bound on architectural resilience; learned scoring methods would likely yield better retention.\n38\t\n39\tOFT~\\cite{kim2025openoft} retains OpenVLA's MLP connector but replaces the action head; its attention entropy (0.667) matches standard OpenVLA (0.661), confirming the connector, not the action head, determines token importance structure. EfficientVLA~\\cite{yang2025efficientvla} corroborates the graded pattern: CogACT~\\cite{li2024cogact} (MLP projector) retains 74.2\\% SR under 78\\% pruning.\n40\t\n41\t\\subsection{Linear Projection: From Immune to Threshold-Graded}\n42\t\\label{sec:linear_results}\n43\t\n44\t$\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B, 256 tokens, linear projection) is evaluated via offline normalized L1 action loss.\\footnote{Offline action loss measures single-step prediction quality; closed-loop evaluation may reveal additional degradation. X-VLA's offline-to-closed-loop gap (Section~\\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}) underscores the need for live verification.} At $k{=}0.1$, the loss is 0.976---a 2.4\\% \\emph{improvement} over the unpruned baseline. At $k{=}0.5$ the loss remains 0.983.\n45\t\n46\tX-VLA~\\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M, 50 tokens) routes vision tokens through a 12-layer BART encoder~\\cite{lewis2020bart} before the LLM. Under L2 norm-based removal, the 99.2\\% baseline holds at $k{=}0.9$ ($97.8 \\pm 0.4$\\%, 3 seeds) but cliffs at $k{=}0.7$ ($57.2 \\pm 1.6$\\%, $-41$pp), then degrades further: $43.7 \\pm 1.0$\\% at $k{=}0.5$ (3 seeds), 23.0\\% at $k{=}0.3$, near-zero at $k{=}0.1$. The threshold lies in the $k{=}0.7$--$0.9$ interval, corresponding to retaining 35--45 of 50 BART-encoded tokens; below this density, cross-token dependencies established by the 12-layer encoder cannot be maintained. The cliff is reproducible across 3 seeds (std 1.6pp) despite extreme per-task variance (cream\\_cheese: $k{=}0.9{=}100$\\% vs.\\ $k{=}0.7{=}0$\\%). That this threshold appears under importance-based removal (the more favorable test) while OpenVLA degrades smoothly under random removal (the harder test) strengthens the architecture-dependent conclusion. The $\\pi_0$--X-VLA contrast shows the connector label alone is insufficient: effective pipeline complexity determines pruning tolerance.\n47\t\n48\t\\subsection{VTR: Re-Encoding Overcomes Cliff-Edge}\n49\t\\label{sec:smart_compress_results}\n50\t\n51\tTo test whether learned re-encoding can overcome the pixel-shuffle cliff-edge, we insert a 22.2M-parameter cross-attention module (Vision Token Re-encoder, VTR) between the vision connector and the LLM backbone (details in Appendix~\\ref{app:smart_compress_details}). VTR compresses 128 input tokens to $M$ output tokens. Both VTR and the baseline train from the same SmolVLA-Base checkpoint for 100K steps with identical hyperparameters. An extended training control (same procedure without the compressor) achieves 45.0\\% SR ($p > 0.9$ vs.\\ the 44.8\\% baseline), which confirms the gain is architectural, not from additional optimization steps.\\footnote{The extended baseline also collapses to 0\\% SR under pruning ($k{=}0.50$, random, true removal), which confirms that additional training does not mitigate the pixel-shuffle vulnerability.}\n52\t\n53\t\\begin{table}[t]\n54\t\\centering\n55\t\\small\n56\t\\caption{VTR re-encoding vs.\\ deletion on SmolVLA (LIBERO-Object). The M-curve is flat: $M{=}16$ (87.5\\% compression) matches $M{=}128$ (no compression) within 1.1pp. A parameter-matched MLP adapter achieves comparable SR ($p = 0.15$ vs.\\ cross-attention). $^\\ddagger$Mean $\\pm$ std over 3 seeds (500 episodes each). Other rows: single seed with Wilson 95\\% CI.}\n57\t\\label{tab:smart_compress}\n58\t\\begin{tabular}{lccrr}\n59\t\\toprule\n60\tMethod & $M$ & Red. & SR (\\%) & Seeds \\\\\n61\t\\midrule\n62\t\\textit{Baseline} & 128 & 0\\% & 44.8 & 1 \\\\\n63\t\\textit{Ext.\\ training} & -- & -- & 45.0 & 1 \\\\\n64\t\\textit{Hard pruning} & -- & 50--90\\% & 0.0 & mult. \\\\\n65\t\\midrule\n66\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 16 & 87.5\\% & 56.9$\\pm$1.3 & 55.8/58.4/56.5 \\\\\n67\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 32 & 75\\% & 60.1$\\pm$1.3 & 61.6/59.2/59.6 \\\\\n68\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 64 & 50\\% & \\textbf{62.0}$\\pm$2.3 & 61.6/60.0/64.4 \\\\\n69\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 96 & 25\\% & 54.5$\\pm$3.2 & 52.6/58.2/52.8 \\\\\n70\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 128 & 0\\% & 58.0$\\pm$3.5 & 59.8/54.0/60.2 \\\\\n71\tMLP adapter$^\\ddagger$ & 32 & 75\\% & 63.4$\\pm$2.6 & 65.2/64.6/60.4 \\\\\n72\t\\midrule\n73\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textit{Official checkpoint (59.2\\% baseline)}} \\\\\n74\tCross-attn & 32 & 75\\% & 53.4 & 1 \\\\\n75\t\\bottomrule\n76\t\\end{tabular}\n77\t\\end{table}\n78\t\n79\tThe M-curve is flat (Table~\\ref{tab:smart_compress}): $M{=}16$ (56.9\\%, 87.5\\% compression) performs within 1.1pp of $M{=}128$ (58.0\\%, no compression), and all five $M$ values cluster within 5pp. A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) achieves 63.4\\% $\\pm$ 2.6 at $M{=}32$ ($p = 0.15$ vs.\\ cross-attention), confirming re-encoding itself, not the attention mechanism, is the operative factor (Figure~\\ref{fig:results_combined}b). Cross-suite evaluation confirms generalization: 53.2\\% on LIBERO-Spatial and 56.4\\% on LIBERO-Goal where hard pruning yields 0\\% (Appendix~\\ref{app:smart_compress_full}). Applying VTR to OpenVLA yields 0\\% SR even in passthrough mode ($M{=}128$), indicating VTR is a targeted remedy for pixel-shuffle entanglement, not a universal compression module.\n80\t\n81\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n82\t\\centering\n83\t\\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\linewidth}\n84\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_degradation_curve.pdf}\n85\t\\caption{Token pruning across connector classes.}\n86\t\\label{fig:degradation_curve}\n87\t\\end{subfigure}\n88\t\\hfill\n89\t\\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\linewidth}\n90\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_compression_curve.pdf}\n91\t\\caption{VTR M-curve on SmolVLA.}\n92\t\\label{fig:compression_curve}\n93\t\\end{subfigure}\n94\t\\caption{Architecture-dependent response to token reduction on LIBERO-Object. (a)~Token pruning across pipeline types: pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) is immune, linear+encoder (X-VLA) is threshold-graded (onset at $k{=}0.7$), MLP projector (OpenVLA) degrades gracefully, pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA) collapses. (b)~VTR M-curve: re-encoding achieves near-lossless compression ($M{=}16 \\approx M{=}128$) where deletion yields 0\\%.}\n95\t\\label{fig:results_combined}\n96\t\\end{figure}\n97\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016qTfa5WJ7EXvtPwdsm26dN","session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885","uuid":"d3316a0f-7c05-4dd0-ab57-57f84f6801ac","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:46:13.554Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012anjCgz6PvfLeczQvy3QWN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TVHfHT9rZJfyGh37iYNgYg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. \n\nCoRL rating scale (1-10): 1=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 5=borderline reject, 6=borderline accept, 8=strong accept, 10=top 5%. Acceptance rate ~25-30%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper systematically analyzes why inference-time vision token pruning works on some VLA architectures but catastrophically fails on others. Testing 5 VLA models (π0 3.3B, X-VLA 880M, OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B, SmolVLA 450M) spanning 3 vision connector families on LIBERO, they find:\n1. Pure linear (π0): pruning-immune (loss improves 2.4% at 90% removal)\n2. Linear + BART 12L (X-VLA): threshold-graded (97.6% SR at k=0.9, cliff to 56.2% at k=0.7)\n3. 3-layer MLP (OpenVLA): smooth graded degradation (-49% relative at 50% removal)\n4. Pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA): catastrophic (0% SR at 5% removal)\n\nCV of token importance scores predicts this spectrum (0.038→0.151→0.215→0.314). For pixel-shuffle, they propose VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding, ~22M params) recovering 0%→60%+ SR at 75% compression. VTR fails on MLP-projector (0% SR), reinforcing connector-dependency.\n\n## Key Related Work Cited\n- RT-2 [1] (Brohan et al., CoRL 2023)\n- OpenVLA [2] (Kim et al., 2024)\n- π0 [3] (Black et al., 2024)\n- X-VLA [4] (Zhen et al., 2024)\n- SmolVLA [5] (HuggingFace, 2025)\n- LIBERO [6] (Liu et al., NeurIPS 2024)\n- SigLIP [7] (Zhai et al., ICCV 2023)\n- LightVLA [8] (ICRA 2026) — token pruning on MLP-projector VLAs\n- See What Matters [9] (ICML 2026) — importance-aware pruning for VLAs\n\nAlso mentioned without citation: Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, EfficientVLA, VLA-IAP, CogACT\n\n## Section Structure\n- Section 1: Introduction (1.5 pages)\n- Section 2: Background and Related Work (0.7 pages, split into 2.1 VLA Models and 2.2 Token Efficiency)\n- Sections 3-7: Setup, Results, Analysis, Discussion, Conclusion\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**\n   - Is the research question novel? (\"Why does pruning work on some VLAs but not others?\")\n   - Is the connector-centric taxonomy a genuine contribution?\n   - Is CV as a zero-cost predictor novel and useful?\n   - Is VTR novel as a method, or is it a straightforward application of cross-attention compression?\n   - How does this compare to concurrent work (LightVLA, See What Matters, EfficientVLA, etc.)?\n\n2. **Related work coverage**\n   - Is the related work section adequate for CoRL?\n   - Are key concurrent/prior works on VLA token efficiency properly discussed?\n   - Missing references: token merging (ToMe), attention pruning in VLMs (FastV, etc.), general vision token reduction literature?\n   - Are the references accurate? (Note: [8] LightVLA ICRA 2026 and [9] See What Matters ICML 2026 — verify plausibility)\n   - Is the positioning fair to concurrent work?\n\n3. **Contribution clarity**\n   - Are the 4 claimed contributions clearly articulated?\n   - Is the paper more \"analysis/diagnosis\" or \"method\"? Is the balance right for CoRL?\n   - Does the paper adequately differentiate from \"yet another pruning paper\"?\n\n4. **Narrative and framing**\n   - Title: appropriate for content?\n   - Abstract: accurate summary? Over-claiming?\n   - \"First systematic analysis\" claim — is this justified?\n   - The connector-dependency narrative — is it convincingly argued?\n   - Does the paper make the case that this matters for the robotics community specifically (CoRL relevance)?\n\n5. **Scope and significance**\n   - Is this a \"nice to know\" finding or does it change how people build/deploy VLAs?\n   - Would practitioners actually use CV as a diagnostic?\n   - Is VTR practically useful beyond the specific SmolVLA case?\n   - What is the lasting impact of this work?\n\n6. **Concerns about reference quality**\n   - References [8] and [9] cite 2026 venues — are these plausible or potentially fabricated?\n   - Reference [5] \"HuggingFace. SmolVLA...\" is informal\n   - Are there missing key references in the VLA compression / token efficiency space?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Key papers or areas that should be discussed]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, framing, or reference issues]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: [1-4, where 4=excellent]\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Related Work Coverage: [1-4]\n- Overall Recommendation: [1-10 CoRL scale]\n- Confidence: [1-5, where 5=expert]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31626,"cache_read_input_tokens":32333,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31626},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885","uuid":"84a334a3-875b-48ff-98d5-28b49849431c"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a4f63fb20d63db3a3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TVHfHT9rZJfyGh37iYNgYg","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. \n\nCoRL rating scale (1-10): 1=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 5=borderline reject, 6=borderline accept, 8=strong accept, 10=top 5%. Acceptance rate ~25-30%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper systematically analyzes why inference-time vision token pruning works on some VLA architectures but catastrophically fails on others. Testing 5 VLA models (π0 3.3B, X-VLA 880M, OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B, SmolVLA 450M) spanning 3 vision connector families on LIBERO, they find:\n1. Pure linear (π0): pruning-immune (loss improves 2.4% at 90% removal)\n2. Linear + BART 12L (X-VLA): threshold-graded (97.6% SR at k=0.9, cliff to 56.2% at k=0.7)\n3. 3-layer MLP (OpenVLA): smooth graded degradation (-49% relative at 50% removal)\n4. Pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA): catastrophic (0% SR at 5% removal)\n\nCV of token importance scores predicts this spectrum (0.038→0.151→0.215→0.314). For pixel-shuffle, they propose VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding, ~22M params) recovering 0%→60%+ SR at 75% compression. VTR fails on MLP-projector (0% SR), reinforcing connector-dependency.\n\n## Key Related Work Cited\n- RT-2 [1] (Brohan et al., CoRL 2023)\n- OpenVLA [2] (Kim et al., 2024)\n- π0 [3] (Black et al., 2024)\n- X-VLA [4] (Zhen et al., 2024)\n- SmolVLA [5] (HuggingFace, 2025)\n- LIBERO [6] (Liu et al., NeurIPS 2024)\n- SigLIP [7] (Zhai et al., ICCV 2023)\n- LightVLA [8] (ICRA 2026) — token pruning on MLP-projector VLAs\n- See What Matters [9] (ICML 2026) — importance-aware pruning for VLAs\n\nAlso mentioned without citation: Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, EfficientVLA, VLA-IAP, CogACT\n\n## Section Structure\n- Section 1: Introduction (1.5 pages)\n- Section 2: Background and Related Work (0.7 pages, split into 2.1 VLA Models and 2.2 Token Efficiency)\n- Sections 3-7: Setup, Results, Analysis, Discussion, Conclusion\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**\n   - Is the research question novel? (\"Why does pruning work on some VLAs but not others?\")\n   - Is the connector-centric taxonomy a genuine contribution?\n   - Is CV as a zero-cost predictor novel and useful?\n   - Is VTR novel as a method, or is it a straightforward application of cross-attention compression?\n   - How does this compare to concurrent work (LightVLA, See What Matters, EfficientVLA, etc.)?\n\n2. **Related work coverage**\n   - Is the related work section adequate for CoRL?\n   - Are key concurrent/prior works on VLA token efficiency properly discussed?\n   - Missing references: token merging (ToMe), attention pruning in VLMs (FastV, etc.), general vision token reduction literature?\n   - Are the references accurate? (Note: [8] LightVLA ICRA 2026 and [9] See What Matters ICML 2026 — verify plausibility)\n   - Is the positioning fair to concurrent work?\n\n3. **Contribution clarity**\n   - Are the 4 claimed contributions clearly articulated?\n   - Is the paper more \"analysis/diagnosis\" or \"method\"? Is the balance right for CoRL?\n   - Does the paper adequately differentiate from \"yet another pruning paper\"?\n\n4. **Narrative and framing**\n   - Title: appropriate for content?\n   - Abstract: accurate summary? Over-claiming?\n   - \"First systematic analysis\" claim — is this justified?\n   - The connector-dependency narrative — is it convincingly argued?\n   - Does the paper make the case that this matters for the robotics community specifically (CoRL relevance)?\n\n5. **Scope and significance**\n   - Is this a \"nice to know\" finding or does it change how people build/deploy VLAs?\n   - Would practitioners actually use CV as a diagnostic?\n   - Is VTR practically useful beyond the specific SmolVLA case?\n   - What is the lasting impact of this work?\n\n6. **Concerns about reference quality**\n   - References [8] and [9] cite 2026 venues — are these plausible or potentially fabricated?\n   - Reference [5] \"HuggingFace. SmolVLA...\" is informal\n   - Are there missing key references in the VLA compression / token efficiency space?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Key papers or areas that should be discussed]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, framing, or reference issues]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: [1-4, where 4=excellent]\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Related Work Coverage: [1-4]\n- Overall Recommendation: [1-10 CoRL scale]\n- Confidence: [1-5, where 5=expert]\n```\n","uuid":"4c9089ce-875f-4f10-8eee-370e8242691c","session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885"}
