{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgLmsE9YCjrBfHUMn75uyb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\appendix\n2\t\n3\t\\section{SmolVLA Pruning Ablation}\n4\t\\label{app:smolvla_ablation}\n5\t\n6\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n7\t\\centering\n8\t\\small\n9\t\\caption{SmolVLA pruning ablation on LIBERO-Object. All configurations collapse to 0\\% SR. The official checkpoint (59.2\\% baseline) shows the same cliff-edge.}\n10\t\\label{tab:smolvla_ablation}\n11\t\\begin{tabular}{llccrr}\n12\t\\toprule\n13\tScoring & $k$ & Mechanism & SR (\\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\\% CI \\\\\n14\t\\midrule\n15\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Baseline (bypass)}} & -- & 44.8 & 500 & [40.5, 49.2] \\\\\n16\t\\midrule\n17\tACIS & 0.50 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n18\tACIS & 0.70 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n19\tACIS & 0.90 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n20\tRandom & 0.95 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n21\tRandom & 0.50 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n22\tACIS & 0.50 & True removal & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.80] \\\\\n23\tACIS & 0.50 & Merge (ToMe) & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n24\t\\midrule\n25\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{Official checkpoint (HuggingFaceVLA/smolvla\\_libero)}} \\\\\n26\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Baseline (bypass)}} & -- & 59.2 & 500 & [54.8, 63.4] \\\\\n27\tRandom & 0.50 & True removal & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n28\t\\bottomrule\n29\t\\end{tabular}\n30\t\\end{table}\n31\t\n32\t\n33\t\\section{Per-Task Breakdown Tables}\n34\t\\label{app:per_task}\n35\t\n36\t\\subsection{SmolVLA Baseline Per-Task Results}\n37\t\n38\tTable~\\ref{tab:smolvla_baseline_pertask} reports per-task success rates for the SmolVLA baseline (bypass mode, no pruning) on LIBERO-Object. All pruning conditions (Table~\\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation}) produce 0\\% success rate on every task; we omit per-task breakdowns for pruning conditions as they carry no additional information beyond the aggregate 0\\% already reported.\n39\t\n40\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n41\t\\centering\n42\t\\small\n43\t\\caption{SmolVLA baseline (bypass mode) per-task success rates on LIBERO-Object (50 episodes per task, 500 total). Overall SR: 44.8\\%.}\n44\t\\label{tab:smolvla_baseline_pertask}\n45\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n46\t\\toprule\n47\tTask & SR (\\%) \\\\\n48\t\\midrule\n49\talphabet\\_soup & 22 \\\\\n50\tbbq\\_sauce & 60 \\\\\n51\tbutter & 42 \\\\\n52\tchocolate\\_pudding & 38 \\\\\n53\tcream\\_cheese & 58 \\\\\n54\tketchup & 26 \\\\\n55\tmilk & 62 \\\\\n56\torange\\_juice & 38 \\\\\n57\tsalad\\_dressing & 50 \\\\\n58\ttomato\\_sauce & 52 \\\\\n59\t\\midrule\n60\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{44.8} \\\\\n61\t\\bottomrule\n62\t\\end{tabular}\n63\t\\end{table}\n64\t\n65\t\\subsection{Official SmolVLA Checkpoint Per-Task Results}\n66\t\n67\tTable~\\ref{tab:smolvla_official_pertask} reports per-task success rates for the official SmolVLA LIBERO checkpoint (HuggingFaceVLA/smolvla\\_libero). Under 50\\% random token removal, all tasks produce 0\\% SR, identical to the fine-tuned checkpoint results.\n68\t\n69\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n70\t\\centering\n71\t\\small\n72\t\\caption{Official SmolVLA checkpoint per-task success rates on LIBERO-Object (50 episodes per task, 500 total). Overall SR: 59.2\\%. Under 50\\% random pruning, all tasks collapse to 0\\%.}\n73\t\\label{tab:smolvla_official_pertask}\n74\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n75\t\\toprule\n76\tTask & Baseline SR (\\%) & $k{=}0.5$ SR (\\%) \\\\\n77\t\\midrule\n78\talphabet\\_soup & 64 & 0 \\\\\n79\tbbq\\_sauce & 38 & 0 \\\\\n80\tbutter & 78 & 0 \\\\\n81\tchocolate\\_pudding & 90 & 0 \\\\\n82\tcream\\_cheese & 40 & 0 \\\\\n83\tketchup & 60 & 0 \\\\\n84\tmilk & 52 & 0 \\\\\n85\torange\\_juice & 80 & 0 \\\\\n86\tsalad\\_dressing & 44 & 0 \\\\\n87\ttomato\\_sauce & 46 & 0 \\\\\n88\t\\midrule\n89\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{59.2} & \\textbf{0.0} \\\\\n90\t\\bottomrule\n91\t\\end{tabular}\n92\t\\end{table}\n93\t\n94\t\\subsection{OpenVLA Per-Task Results Under Pruning}\n95\t\n96\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n97\t\\centering\n98\t\\small\n99\t\\caption{OpenVLA per-task success rates (\\%) on LIBERO-Object under random token removal across five retention ratios (50 episodes per task). Baseline overall SR: 74.0\\%. Tasks requiring fine-grained discrimination (butter, cream\\_cheese) degrade earliest; visually distinctive targets (orange\\_juice, salad\\_dressing) are most resilient.}\n100\t\\label{tab:openvla_pertask}\n101\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n102\t\\toprule\n103\tTask & $k{=}0.95$ & $k{=}0.90$ & $k{=}0.70$ & $k{=}0.50$ & $k{=}0.30$ \\\\\n104\t\\midrule\n105\talphabet\\_soup & 76 & 70 & 74 & 60 & 14 \\\\\n106\tbbq\\_sauce & 38 & 28 & 46 & 44 & 30 \\\\\n107\tbutter & 84 & 76 & 10 & 2 & 0 \\\\\n108\tchocolate\\_pudding & 54 & 56 & 32 & 40 & 10 \\\\\n109\tcream\\_cheese & 84 & 78 & 18 & 8 & 0 \\\\\n110\tketchup & 82 & 70 & 68 & 50 & 30 \\\\\n111\tmilk & 46 & 42 & 40 & 24 & 8 \\\\\n112\torange\\_juice & 74 & 60 & 76 & 72 & 42 \\\\\n113\tsalad\\_dressing & 64 & 54 & 64 & 36 & 64 \\\\\n114\ttomato\\_sauce & 92 & 86 & 62 & 38 & 8 \\\\\n115\t\\midrule\n116\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{69.4} & \\textbf{62.0} & \\textbf{49.0} & \\textbf{37.4} & \\textbf{20.6} \\\\\n117\t\\bottomrule\n118\t\\end{tabular}\n119\t\\end{table}\n120\t\n121\t\\subsection{OpenVLA Full Degradation Curve}\n122\t\n123\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n124\t\\centering\n125\t\\small\n126\t\\caption{OpenVLA success rates on LIBERO-Object under random token removal at six retention ratios (50 episodes per task, 500 total per ratio). Baseline SR: 74.0\\%. All confidence intervals are Wilson 95\\%.}\n127\t\\label{tab:openvla_full}\n128\t\\begin{tabular}{ccrr}\n129\t\\toprule\n130\t$k$ & Tokens Retained & SR (\\%) & Wilson 95\\% CI \\\\\n131\t\\midrule\n132\t1.00 (baseline) & 256 & 74.0 & [69.9, 77.7] \\\\\n133\t0.95 & 243 & 69.4 & [65.2, 73.3] \\\\\n134\t0.90 & 230 & 62.0 & [57.7, 66.2] \\\\\n135\t0.70 & 179 & 49.0 & [44.6, 53.4] \\\\\n136\t0.50 & 128 & 37.4 & [33.3, 41.7] \\\\\n137\t0.30 & 77 & 20.6 & [17.2, 24.4] \\\\\n138\t\\bottomrule\n139\t\\end{tabular}\n140\t\\end{table}\n141\t\n142\t\\subsection{Smart Compress Per-Task Comparison}\n143\t\n144\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n145\t\\centering\n146\t\\small\n147\t\\caption{Smart Compress per-task success rates on LIBERO-Object for $M \\in \\{32, 64, 128\\}$ (seed 42; 3-seed means are reported in Table~\\ref{tab:smart_compress}). All three achieve similar overall SR (59.8\\%--61.6\\%), but individual task performance varies substantially across configurations, with per-task differences up to 30 percentage points.}\n148\t\\label{tab:smart_compress_pertask}\n149\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n150\t\\toprule\n151\tTask & $M{=}32$ (\\%) & $M{=}64$ (\\%) & $M{=}128$ (\\%) \\\\\n152\t\\midrule\n153\talphabet\\_soup & 68 & 74 & 62 \\\\\n154\tbbq\\_sauce & 46 & 56 & 34 \\\\\n155\tbutter & 82 & 84 & 82 \\\\\n156\tchocolate\\_pudding & 90 & 74 & 82 \\\\\n157\tcream\\_cheese & 64 & 38 & 58 \\\\\n158\tketchup & 66 & 64 & 62 \\\\\n159\tmilk & 76 & 62 & 56 \\\\\n160\torange\\_juice & 28 & 46 & 58 \\\\\n161\tsalad\\_dressing & 60 & 64 & 54 \\\\\n162\ttomato\\_sauce & 36 & 54 & 50 \\\\\n163\t\\midrule\n164\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{61.6} & \\textbf{61.6} & \\textbf{59.8} \\\\\n165\t\\bottomrule\n166\t\\end{tabular}\n167\t\\end{table}\n168\t\n169\t\\subsection{Smart Compress Multi-Seed Results}\n170\t\n171\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n172\t\\centering\n173\t\\small\n174\t\\caption{Smart Compress success rates across multiple seeds on LIBERO-Object (500 episodes each). Mean $\\pm$ std reported. $M{=}128$ has two seeds; others have three.}\n175\t\\label{tab:multi_seed}\n176\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccr}\n177\t\\toprule\n178\t$M$ & Seed 42 & Seed 43 & Seed 44 & Mean $\\pm$ Std \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\t32 & 61.6 & 59.2 & 59.6 & 60.1 $\\pm$ 1.3 \\\\\n181\t64 & 61.6 & 60.0 & 64.4 & 62.0 $\\pm$ 2.3 \\\\\n182\t96 & 52.6 & 58.2 & 52.8 & 54.5 $\\pm$ 3.2 \\\\\n183\t128 & 59.8 & -- & 60.2 & 60.0 $\\pm$ 0.3 \\\\\n184\t\\bottomrule\n185\t\\end{tabular}\n186\t\\end{table}\n187\t\n188\t\n189\t\\section{Full Smart Compress Ablation}\n190\t\\label{app:smart_compress_full}\n191\t\n192\tTable~\\ref{tab:smart_compress_full} reports all five Smart Compress configurations. The cross-attention module improves performance regardless of compression: $M \\in \\{32, 64, 128\\}$ all achieve $\\sim$60\\% SR, exceeding the 44.8\\% baseline by 15--17 points ($p < 0.001$, two-proportion $z$-test). Only at $M = 16$ (87.5\\% compression) does performance decline modestly to 55.8\\%, still 11 points above baseline. The $M{=}96$ dip (mean 54.5\\% across three seeds, Table~\\ref{tab:multi_seed}) may reflect an intermediate bottleneck that introduces representational redundancy without the regularization benefit of stronger compression ($M{=}32$, $M{=}64$).\n193\t\n194\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_suite} reports performance across task suites. All suites are in-distribution: the training set (HuggingFaceVLA/libero) covers Object, Spatial, Goal, and Long. On LIBERO-Spatial, Smart Compress achieves 53.2\\% SR (vs.\\ 0\\% under hard pruning); on LIBERO-Goal, 56.4\\% SR ($-6.6$pp from the 63.0\\% unpruned baseline, vs.\\ 0\\% under pruning). The LIBERO-Goal decline indicates that re-encoding benefits vary across task structures even within the training distribution. Per-task results are in Table~\\ref{tab:smart_compress_pertask}.\n195\t\n196\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n197\t\\centering\n198\t\\small\n199\t\\caption{Full Smart Compress ablation on LIBERO-Object across five compression levels. $M{=}32$ and $M{=}64$ achieve similar SR ($\\sim$60\\%); $M{=}96$ shows a modest dip; the $M{=}128$ passthrough confirms the gain originates from re-encoding.}\n200\t\\label{tab:smart_compress_full}\n201\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccrr}\n202\t\\toprule\n203\tMethod & $M$ & Reduction & SR (\\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\\% CI \\\\\n204\t\\midrule\n205\t\\textit{Baseline} & 128 & 0\\% & 44.8 & 500 & [40.5, 49.2] \\\\\n206\t\\textit{Extended training} & -- & -- & 45.0 & 500 & [40.7, 49.3] \\\\\n207\t\\textit{Hard pruning} & -- & 50--90\\% & 0.0 & 500+ & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n208\t\\midrule\n209\tSmart Compress & 128 & 0\\% & 60.0$^\\dagger$ & 1000 & $\\pm$0.3 \\\\\n210\tSmart Compress & 96 & 25\\% & 52.6 & 500 & [48.2, 56.9] \\\\\n211\tSmart Compress & 64 & 50\\% & 61.6 & 500 & [57.3, 65.8] \\\\\n212\tSmart Compress & 32 & 75\\% & 61.6 & 500 & [57.3, 65.8] \\\\\n213\tSmart Compress & 16 & 87.5\\% & 55.8 & 500 & [51.4, 60.1] \\\\\n214\t\\bottomrule\n215\t\\end{tabular}\n216\t\n217\t\\vspace{2pt}\n218\t{\\footnotesize $^\\dagger$Mean $\\pm$ std over 2 seeds (500 episodes each). Other Smart Compress rows report single seed (seed 42) with Wilson 95\\% CI. See Table~\\ref{tab:multi_seed} for full multi-seed results.}\n219\t\\end{table}\n220\t\n221\t\n222\t\\section{Smart Compress Evaluation Across Task Suites}\n223\t\\label{app:cross_suite}\n224\t\n225\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_suite} reports the performance of Smart Compress ($M{=}32$) across three LIBERO suites. All suites are in-distribution: the training set (HuggingFaceVLA/libero) covers Object, Spatial, Goal, and Long (40 tasks, 1693 episodes total). On LIBERO-Goal, performance drops from 63.0\\% to 56.4\\% ($-6.6$pp). On LIBERO-Spatial, Smart Compress achieves 53.2\\% SR, where the official SmolVLA checkpoint scores 43.8\\% and hard pruning yields near-zero (0.6\\%). While 53.2\\% nominally exceeds the official Spatial baseline, this difference concentrates in two of ten tasks (+44pp and +48pp respectively); the remaining eight tasks show a net difference of only +0.2pp. We therefore do not treat the Spatial comparison as evidence that re-encoding improves over the uncompressed official baseline. The qualitative conclusion holds regardless: the cliff-edge pattern and re-encoding recovery generalize across task structures within the training distribution. The LIBERO-Goal decline indicates that re-encoding benefits vary across task structures.\n226\t\n227\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n228\t\\centering\n229\t\\small\n230\t\\caption{Smart Compress ($M{=}32$) evaluation across LIBERO task suites. All suites are in-distribution (training covers Object, Spatial, Goal, Long). Hard pruning (any method, $k{=}0.50$) produces 0\\% or near-zero on all suites.}\n231\t\\label{tab:cross_suite}\n232\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n233\t\\toprule\n234\tSuite & Baseline (unpruned) & Smart Compress $M{=}32$ & Hard Pruning $k{=}0.50$ \\\\\n235\t\\midrule\n236\tObject & 44.8\\% & 61.6\\% & 0\\% \\\\\n237\tSpatial & 51.8\\% & 53.2\\% & 0.6\\% \\\\\n238\tGoal & 63.0\\% & 56.4\\% & 0\\% \\\\\n239\t\\bottomrule\n240\t\\end{tabular}\n241\t\\end{table}\n242\t\n243\t\n244\t\\section{Smart Compress Training Details}\n245\t\\label{app:smart_compress_details}\n246\t\n247\tThe Smart Compress module is a cross-attention pooling layer inserted between SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle vision connector and the Qwen2-0.5B language backbone. It consists of $M$ learned query vectors that attend over the 128 vision tokens through standard multi-head cross-attention, producing $M$ compressed tokens as input to the LLM. The compressor adds 22.2M trainable parameters (approximately 5\\% of SmolVLA's 451.5M total). Training is end-to-end: compressor and VLA policy parameters are jointly optimized.\n248\t\n249\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n250\t\\centering\n251\t\\small\n252\t\\caption{Smart Compress training hyperparameters. All configurations ($M \\in \\{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\\}$) use identical procedures; only the number of query vectors differs.}\n253\t\\label{tab:training_hyperparams}\n254\t\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n255\t\\toprule\n256\tHyperparameter & Value \\\\\n257\t\\midrule\n258\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Model}} \\\\\n259\t\\quad Base checkpoint & SmolVLA-Base (pretrained, before LIBERO fine-tuning) \\\\\n260\t\\quad Compressor architecture & Cross-attention, 2 layers, 12 heads, dropout 0.1 \\\\\n261\t\\quad Compressor parameters & 22.2M \\\\\n262\t\\quad Total trainable parameters & 122.1M / 472.2M (25.85\\%) \\\\\n263\t\\midrule\n264\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Optimization}} \\\\\n265\t\\quad Optimizer & AdamW ($\\beta_1{=}0.9$, $\\beta_2{=}0.95$, weight decay $10^{-10}$) \\\\\n266\t\\quad Learning rate & $10^{-4}$ \\\\\n267\t\\quad Schedule & Linear warmup (1K steps, start factor 0.01) $\\to$ cosine \\\\\n268\t\\quad & annealing ($\\eta_{\\min}{=}2.5 \\times 10^{-6}$) \\\\\n269\t\\quad Max gradient norm & 10.0 \\\\\n270\t\\quad Total training steps & 100,000 \\\\\n271\t\\midrule\n272\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Data \\& batching}} \\\\\n273\t\\quad Dataset & HuggingFaceVLA/libero (40 tasks, 1693 episodes across \\\\\n274\t\\quad & Object, Spatial, Goal, Long suites) \\\\\n275\t\\quad Batch size & 4 (no gradient accumulation)$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n276\t\\quad Num workers & 4 \\\\\n277\t\\quad Eval interval & every 5K steps \\\\\n278\t\\quad Checkpoint interval & every 1K steps \\\\\n279\t\\midrule\n280\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Hardware \\& runtime}} \\\\\n281\t\\quad Hardware & 1$\\times$ NVIDIA RTX 4090 D (24GB) \\\\\n282\t\\quad Precision & fp32 (no mixed precision) \\\\\n283\t\\quad Wall clock & $\\sim$6h 43m per configuration \\\\\n284\t\\quad Training speed & 4.1 samples/sec \\\\\n285\t\\quad Seed & 42 (seeds 43, 44 for multi-seed runs) \\\\\n286\t\\midrule\n287\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{$M=32$ final metrics}} \\\\\n288\t\\quad Train action loss & 0.11 \\\\\n289\t\\quad Eval action loss & 0.052 \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{$M=64$ final metrics}} \\\\\n292\t\\quad Train action loss & 0.045 \\\\\n293\t\\quad Eval action loss & 0.062 \\\\\n294\t\\bottomrule\n295\t\\end{tabular}\n296\t\n297\t\\vspace{2pt}\n298\t{\\footnotesize $^\\dagger$The official SmolVLA checkpoint uses batch size 64 across 8 GPUs (effective batch size 512). Our batch size 4 on a single GPU contributes to the lower baseline SR (44.8\\% vs.\\ 87.3\\%). All Smart Compress configurations and the extended training control use this same batch size for fair comparison.}\n299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\tThe loss profile differs between configurations: $M=32$ has higher train loss (0.11 vs.\\ 0.045) but lower eval loss (0.052 vs.\\ 0.062), consistent with the smaller bottleneck acting as a stronger regularizer. Despite this loss difference, both achieve identical closed-loop success rates (61.6\\%), reinforcing the finding that offline loss does not predict closed-loop performance (Section~\\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}).\n302\t\n303\t\\subsection{MLP Adapter Control}\n304\t\\label{app:mlp_adapter}\n305\t\n306\tTo verify that re-encoding effectiveness is not specific to cross-attention, we train a parameter-matched MLP adapter as a control. The adapter is a 3-layer feed-forward network with expansion ratio 4 (hidden dimension $4 \\times d_{\\text{model}}$), using average pooling for token reduction from 128 to $M$ tokens. Total parameters: 22.14M, matched to the cross-attention module's 22.2M. Training follows the identical protocol as Smart Compress: SmolVLA-Base checkpoint, 100K steps, learning rate $10^{-4}$, batch size 4, same LIBERO dataset. At $M = 32$ (75\\% reduction), the MLP adapter achieves $63.4 \\pm 2.6$\\% SR over 3 seeds, statistically indistinguishable from the cross-attention result ($60.1 \\pm 1.3$\\%; two-sample $t$-test, $p = 0.15$). This confirms that the re-encoding benefit does not depend on the specific module architecture.\n307\t\n308\t\n309\t\\section{Offline Single-Step Action Error Analysis}\n310\t\\label{app:offline_error}\n311\t\n312\tTable~\\ref{tab:offline_action_error} reports the single-step action prediction error under pruning, computed over 200 evaluation frames from LIBERO-Object. The unpruned model (bypass mode) produces actions with an L2 distance of 0.414 from ground truth; under ACIS pruning at $k{=}0.50$, this increases to 1.391 ($3.4\\times$). The per-dimension breakdown of the pruned-vs-unpruned difference reveals that the gripper dimension dominates (L2 = 1.02), corresponding to complete gripper state inversion on many frames. Position errors (0.20--0.30 per axis) are moderate individually but compound across the 7-dimensional action space. These single-step errors are large enough to push the robot off-trajectory within a few steps, explaining why 0\\% of pruned episodes succeed in closed-loop evaluation despite moderate average offline metrics.\n313\t\n314\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n315\t\\centering\n316\t\\small\n317\t\\caption{Single-step action prediction error (SmolVLA, $k{=}0.50$, ACIS, zero-mask, 200 LIBERO-Object evaluation frames). Pruning increases action error by $3.4\\times$ and introduces catastrophic gripper inversions.}\n318\t\\label{tab:offline_action_error}\n319\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n320\t\\toprule\n321\tMetric & Unpruned & Pruned ($k{=}0.50$) \\\\\n322\t\\midrule\n323\tAction L2 vs.\\ ground truth & 0.414 & 1.391 \\\\\n324\tPruned vs.\\ unpruned L2 & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{1.284} \\\\\n325\tRelative action error & -- & 111.5\\% \\\\\n326\t\\midrule\n327\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textit{Per-dimension L2 (pruned vs.\\ unpruned)}} \\\\\n328\t\\quad Position $(x, y, z)$ & -- & 0.20, 0.25, 0.30 \\\\\n329\t\\quad Rotation $(r_x, r_y, r_z)$ & -- & 0.01, 0.02, 0.01 \\\\\n330\t\\quad Gripper & -- & 1.02 \\\\\n331\t\\bottomrule\n332\t\\end{tabular}\n333\t\\end{table}\n334\t\n335\t\n336\t\\section{Supplementary Figures}\n337\t\\label{app:supplementary_figures}\n338\t\n339\t\\begin{figure}[ht]\n340\t\\centering\n341\t\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_pixel_shuffle.pdf}\n342\t\\caption{Pixel-shuffle folding: each $4{\\times}4$ block of SigLIP patches is channel-concatenated into one token. Removing any token eliminates its receptive field (gray) with no redundancy.}\n343\t\\label{fig:pixel_shuffle}\n344\t\\end{figure}\n345\t\n346\t\\begin{figure}[ht]\n347\t\\centering\n348\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_entropy_layers.pdf}\n349\t\\caption{Per-layer attention entropy ratio for SmolVLA (16 layers), OpenVLA (32 layers), and $\\pi_0$ (18 layers). SmolVLA and $\\pi_0$ maintain near-uniform attention throughout, while OpenVLA progressively sharpens from 0.98 to 0.48. Despite similar entropy, SmolVLA and $\\pi_0$ exhibit opposite pruning outcomes due to token importance heterogeneity (CV $= 0.314$ vs.\\ $0.038$). X-VLA (entropy $= 0.614$, CV $= 0.151$) occupies an intermediate position, consistent with its threshold-graded pattern (onset at $k{=}0.7$).}\n350\t\\label{fig:entropy_layers}\n351\t\\end{figure}\n352\t\n353\t\n354\t\\section{Attention Entropy Measurement Details}\n355\t\\label{app:entropy_details}\n356\t\n357\tWe measure the attention entropy ratio as the normalized Shannon entropy of the attention weights that the action decoder assigns to vision token positions. For a model with $N$ vision tokens, the entropy ratio is:\n358\t\\begin{equation}\n359\tH_{\\text{ratio}} = \\frac{-\\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha_i \\log \\alpha_i}{\\log N}\n360\t\\end{equation}\n361\twhere $\\alpha_i$ is the attention weight on the $i$-th vision token. The ratio ranges from 0 (all attention on one token) to 1 (uniform attention).\n362\t\n363\t\\paragraph{SmolVLA.} Measured on 2400 frames from LIBERO rollouts (overall entropy) and 260 frames across all 10 LIBERO-Object tasks (per-layer analysis across 16 layers). The mean per-layer entropy ratio is 0.95 (range: 0.93 to 0.98), consistent with near-uniform attention across all 128 vision tokens. The per-layer breakdown confirms this uniformity is not an averaging artifact:\n364\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n365\t    \\item All 16 layers: 0.93--0.98 (spread 0.05)\n366\t    \\item Minimum: layer 13 (0.93)\n367\t\\end{itemize}\n368\tThe flat profile across all processing depths confirms that pixel-shuffle tokens resist selective attention at every layer: because each token encodes 16 spatially contiguous patches via channel concatenation, all tokens are comparably information-dense, and the transformer has no basis for developing spatial selectivity.\n369\t\n370\t\\paragraph{$\\pi_0$.} Measured on 100 frames from LIBERO-Object (tasks 20--29) across all 18 layers of the Gemma-based PaliGemma backbone (512 vision tokens: 256 per image $\\times$ 2 cameras). The mean suffix-to-vision entropy ratio is 0.959 (range: 0.931--0.983, std 0.013), near-uniform across all layers with no progressive sharpening:\n371\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n372\t    \\item All 18 layers: 0.931--0.983 (spread 0.052)\n373\t    \\item Minimum: 0.931; Maximum: 0.983\n374\t\\end{itemize}\n375\tThe flat entropy profile, combined with $\\pi_0$'s extremely low CV (0.038), explains its immunity to pruning: tokens carry near-equal importance and receive near-equal attention. Removing any subset is equivalent to uniform spatial downsampling, preserving a proportional share of the visual field without disrupting the attention distribution.\n376\t\n377\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA.} Measured on 250 frames from real LIBERO rollouts. The overall entropy ratio is 0.64. The layer-wise breakdown reveals progressive sharpening:\n378\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n379\t    \\item Shallow layers (0--1): 0.95--0.98\n380\t    \\item Early layers (2--4): 0.70--0.80\n381\t    \\item Mid layers (12--20): 0.57--0.66\n382\t    \\item Deep layers (24--30): 0.48--0.57 (minimum 0.48 at layer 29)\n383\t\\end{itemize}\n384\t12.1\\% of total attention weight falls on vision tokens overall, and within that fraction, the distribution is non-uniform in deeper layers. This progressive concentration indicates that OpenVLA has learned to selectively attend to task-relevant spatial locations, a pattern enabled by the MLP projector's preservation of per-patch spatial granularity.\n385\t\n386\t\\paragraph{Measurement conditions.} All models are measured on real LIBERO rollout data: SmolVLA on 2400 frames (overall entropy) and 260 frames (per-layer analysis), $\\pi_0$ on 100 frames from LIBERO-Object, OpenVLA on 250 frames from 50 episodes $\\times$ 5 initial frames. Sample size differences reflect computational constraints (OpenVLA's 7B forward pass is substantially more expensive), but all measurements use task-relevant visual inputs. Entropy statistics show low variance across frames (std $< 0.02$ for OpenVLA, std $= 0.013$ for $\\pi_0$).\n387\t\n388\t\n389\t\\section{Inference Cost-Benefit Analysis}\n390\t\\label{app:cost_benefit}\n391\t\n392\tThe primary deployment benefit of Smart Compress at this model scale is memory, not latency. Reducing from 128 to 32 vision tokens shrinks the KV-cache footprint for the vision portion by 75\\%, a meaningful reduction for memory-constrained robotic hardware where KV-cache size can limit the number of concurrent policy instances or maximum context length.\n393\t\n394\tEnd-to-end inference latency improves only modestly: 154.78\\,ms at $M{=}32$ versus 164.33\\,ms at full token count, a $1.06\\times$ speedup (6\\% reduction). At $M{=}64$: 156.16\\,ms ($1.05\\times$). The gap between token reduction and latency gain reflects SmolVLA's compact size (451.5M parameters): self-attention over 128 vision tokens is not the inference bottleneck, and the compressor's 22.2M-parameter cross-attention computation partially offsets downstream savings. Token pruning becomes a more compelling latency optimization for VLAs with larger token budgets ($>$256 tokens) or larger backbones (7B+ parameters), where attention cost scales quadratically with sequence length. Whether the training cost (100K steps, 6.5 hours per configuration) is justified depends on deployment context: for VLAs deployed across many tasks, the one-time training cost is amortized; for rapid prototyping, retraining is a barrier.\n395\t\n396\t\\paragraph{Comparison with the official checkpoint.}\n397\tSmart Compress at $M = 128$ (60.0\\% $\\pm$ 0.3 over 2 seeds) achieves performance comparable to the official SmolVLA checkpoint trained with 16$\\times$ larger batch size (59.2\\%, CI [54.8\\%, 63.4\\%]). The overlapping confidence intervals preclude strong conclusions about the equivalence of these mechanisms. This comparison should be interpreted cautiously given the different training conditions (batch size, total compute, optimizer state)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016qTfa5WJ7EXvtPwdsm26dN","session_id":"2be1a920-1f7c-4c94-bc45-d01d7138d885","uuid":"99f28091-6ca0-4d61-9015-ab4fff9f3b27","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:46:20.422Z"}
